disp: NOT_AN_INDEPENDENT_CONSTRAINT
nature: CONFIGURATION_DIRECTIVE (EditorConfig root marker; lookup-boundary semantics defined by the EditorConfig format)
appl: actor=EditorConfig-aware tool (IDE, dotnet format, analyzers); action=stop searching ancestor directories for .editorconfig; trigger=resolving settings for a file under the repository; modality=CONFIGURATION FACT; scope=repository files; exceptions=descendant .editorconfig files still apply; timing=settings resolution; enforcement=tool behaviour, no consumer executed; loading=not an instruction file (never loaded into agent context)
compared: FG-M-10e8af9084 FG-M-065565b797 FG-M-527a265e72
rationale: .editorconfig:4 'root = true' verified. It is a standard configuration marker, not an agent-facing obligation, prohibition or acceptance gate: it only scopes where the already-registered formatting values (FG-M-10e8af9084, FG-M-065565b797; 'follow .editorconfig' FG-M-527a265e72) are looked up. Both Claude runs explicitly read and classified the line as IMPLEMENTATION (M-S001982, M-S001774). The Codex scenario only differs for files outside the template (an ancestor .editorconfig above the repository), so it adds no allowed/forbidden repository content. Not EXTRA-adjudicated.
scenario: N/A as a constraint: with root=false the repository's own formatting values are unchanged; only unspecified properties could be inherited from a directory outside the repository, which no instruction governs.
conf: MEDIUM
open_issue: Methodology: if Stage 2 decides that configuration-inheritance boundaries are registrable constraints, revisit this claim together with the same root=true claims of M1, L0, L1, K0 and K1.
policy_hint: RUN-009 (Codex suggestion; not adjudicated)

@@ CIS-M-0002
disp: EXISTING_GROUP_NEEDS_EXPLICIT_FACET
nature: HOOK_RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR (output channel/severity of the post-tool manifest check after a failed shell command)
appl: actor=dependency-guard PostToolUseFailure hook; action=deliver manifest violations as hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext instead of decision:block; trigger=a failed Bash/PowerShell command leaves manifest violations; modality=ENFORCED_ADVISORY (context, not a block decision); scope=shell commands in the repository; exceptions=successful commands still get decision:block; timing=after the failed tool call; enforcement=static branch verified, host runtime not tested; loading=settings.json hook
match: FG-M-89479fb15b
compared: FG-M-6c4ecd4f31 FG-M-ddd9d93ab6
rationale: hook-io.mjs:85-90 verified with respond() at 74-77 (process.exit(0) after the first write), so for event PostToolUseFailure only the additionalContext payload is emitted; dependency-guard.mjs:171-177 passes the event. FG-M-89479fb15b says violations after 'any other or failed shell command' are 'returned as a blocking reason', which over-states the failed-command case. The facet records the severity/channel boundary; it adds no agent obligation (the comment says the reason still reaches Claude with the error).
scenario: Same manifest violation after a successful versus a failed shell command: decision:block versus additionalContext only.
conf: HIGH
facet: FG-M-89479fb15b | NEW_BEHAVIOR_BOUNDARY | For a failed shell command (PostToolUseFailure) the violation reason is emitted only as hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext, not as decision:block (static reading of hook-io.mjs postToolBlock; host semantics of PostToolUseFailure not tested).

@@ CIS-M-0003
disp: EXISTING_GROUP_NEEDS_EXPLICIT_FACET
nature: HOOK_RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR (silent exit on a clean snapshot)
appl: actor=dependency-guard PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hook; action=exit without notes or owner routing; trigger=initial or re-taken manifest snapshot, no removals, no recognised dependency/reference command, no violations; modality=ENFORCEMENT EXCEPTION; scope=shell commands in the repository; exceptions=a recognised dependency/reference command or a removal is still routed; timing=after the tool call; enforcement=static branch verified, not executed; loading=settings.json hook
match: FG-M-89479fb15b
compared: FG-M-6c4ecd4f31
rationale: dependency-guard.mjs:178-185 verified: 'if (manifests.snapshot && removed.length === 0 && !knownCommand) done();' precedes postToolContext. FG-M-89479fb15b ('otherwise notes and owner routing are added as context') and the Codex group FG-M-6c4ecd4f31 ('adds owner routing') do not state this exception, so the groups are over-general for the snapshot case. This is an enforcement-output boundary, not a new agent rule.
scenario: First shell command in a fresh worktree; the snapshot has manifest notes but no violations: nothing is emitted, whereas a later non-snapshot change with the same notes emits them.
conf: HIGH
facet: FG-M-89479fb15b | NEW_BEHAVIOR_BOUNDARY | When the check is an initial or re-taken snapshot with no removals, no recognised dependency/reference command and no violations, the hook saves state and exits without emitting notes or owner routing.

